On Tue, Apr 14 2026 at 09:52, Thomas Weißschuh (Schneider Electric) wrote:
> Extend the hrtimer subsystem to also handle auxiliary clocks if those
> are enabled.
>
> These hrtimers will be used to implement clock_nanosleep() for
> auxiliary clocks in a later patch.
>
> In contrast to the regular clocks, auxiliary clocks can be disabled at
> runtime. The hrtimer APIs do not allow signalling of failure states,
> so a disabled clock can not be reported to the caller. As hrtimers can
> be reused multiple times it is also not possible to reference-count the
> auxiliary clock and prevent it from being disabled while it has
> associated hrtimers alive. Also a timer should never expire too early,
> which prevents a forced expiration when the clock is disabled.
> Therefore keep the hrtimers running while the clock is disabled and
> expire them when the clock is enabled again and the expiration time
> is reached.
>
> Similar to existing hrtimers, only a simple offset is used to convert
> between auxiliary and monotonic timestamps to reprogram the clocksource.
> If the auxiliary clock runs at a different rate than the monotonic clock
> The expiration will not be exact. This works the same as hrtimres based
> on CLOCK_REALTIME.

That's not really correct.

CLOCK_REALTIME and CLOCK_MONOTONIC are using the same underlying
frequency and conversion factors. They are strictly coupled by the
offset. The only thing what makes timers expire truly late is when
CLOCK_REALTIME is set and jumps forwards. But if the clock is not set
and only steered by NTP/PTP the coupling is always accurate.

You are right, that user space might observe CLOCK_REALTIME going
backwards or jumping forward, but user space can monitor the clock set
events and deal with it. But as long as the clock was not set, the
kernel has to guarantee that timers do not expire early.

AUX clocks can have different frequencies than CLOCK_MONOTONIC. That
means if you only use an offset, which is updated once per tick, then
depending on the frequency ratio, those timers can expire slightly late,
which is fine, but they can also expire early, which violates the
guarantee of clock_nanosleep() that the sleep never comes back early.

Let's assume:

       - HZ=100

       - nominal clock frequency of the clocksource 1GHz

       - Adjustment CLOCK_MONOTONIC -200ppm

       - Adjustment CLOCK_AUX +200ppm

   fMono  = 1GHz - 1Ghz * 200ppm = 0.9998 GHz
   fAux   = 1GHz + 1GHz * 200ppm = 1.0002 GHz

So at the tick when updates happen

   tMono = tMono_base
   tAux  = tAux_base == tmono + offset

which is correct.

Within the tick period, i.e. before the next update, the advancement of
the clocks is:

   tMono = tMono_base + tMono_delta

   tAux  = tAux_base + tAux_delta

Taking the full 10ms of the tick period into account this means based on
CLOCK_MONO:

   tick_delta  = 9998000

   tMono_delta = tick_delta / 0.9998 = 10ms

   tAux_delta  = tick_delta / 1.0002 = 9.99600 ms

That's 4us early, which is not a lot, but trivially to observe in user
space. That means test cases will fail. Even more so with the pending
changes, which validate user controlled timers before enqueuing them
based on ktime_get() and the clock offset, because that does not involve
wakeup and scheduling. We have the same problem for in kernel users,
which also rely on not being expired early.

That's also affected by the asynchronous updates of the various time
keepers.

Who would have thought that having different notions of time is
complicated?

Fortunately there are only a few places where this matters:

    1) hrtimer_check_user_timer() - new in tip

       Trivial to solve. It just requires to read the actual time of the
       clock instead of deriving if from ktim_get() by offset.

    2) hrtimer_interrupt->__hrtimer_run_queues

       That's where the actual prevention of early expiry is required.

    3) hrtimer_update_next_event() (hrtimer_bases_first())

       Needs also to be aware because otherwise a timer which was
       "expired" according to MONO + offset, but prevented in #2, would
       then rearm the timer interrupt based on MONO + offset again
       (which means in the past) until the timer actually expires. IOW,
       4us interrupt storm for nothing.

Let's look at #2:

	for_each_active_base(base, cpu_base, active) {
		ktime_t basenow = ktime_add(now, *base->offset);

it uses basenow to check whether a timer is expired or not. That's
correct for the core clock ids, but not necessarily correct for AUX.

So you could do (But don't do so):

	for_each_active_base(base, cpu_base, active & CORE_MASK) {
		ktime_t basenow = ktime_add(now, *base->offset);
                ...
        }

	for_each_active_base(base, cpu_base, active & AUX_MASK) {
		ktime_t basenow = ktime_add(now, *base->offset);
                ...
                if (basenow < base->expires_next)
                	continue;
                basenow = ktime_get_with_offset(base->clockid);
                if (basenow < base->expires_next)
                	continue;
	}                        

and do something similar in hrtimer_update_next_event(). But that's
expensive as it needs to read time from the hardware, which involves
serializing instructions.

Both invoke hrtimer_update_base() before they start evaluating.
hrtimer_update_base() invokes ktime_get_update_offsets_now(), which
updates the offsets if necessary and as the name suggests reads time,
i.e. CLOCK_MONOTONIC.

__hrtimer_run_queues() gets this time handed in, which allows to do the
following:

	for_each_active_base(base, cpu_base, active & AUX_MASK) {
        	ktime_t basenow = mono_to_aux(now, base->mono_ref);

		if (basenow < base->expires_next)
                	continue;
	}                        

hrtimer_update_next_event() does not have access to 'now', but as there
is always a hrtimer_update_base() invocation close enough to the
invocation of hrtimer_update_next_event() you can store the time
returned from ktime_get_update_offsets_now() in cpu_base and use that
for the computation. Or change the callers to hand it in.

monot_to_aux() does:

        s64 delta_mono_ns, adj;
        unsigned int sft;
        int mult;

        delta_mono_ns = now - mono_ref->mono_base;
        mult = mono_ref->mult;
        sft = mono_ref->shift;
        
        adj = (delta_mono_ns * mult) >> sft;
        return now + adj;

Where does mono_ref come from?

aux_mono_ref[$N] has to be updated when offs_aux[$N] is updated once
per tick in tk_aux_get_mono_offset():

        cycles = read_clock();
        mono_now = tkr_mono.base + cycles_to_ns(tkr_mono, cycles);
        aux_now = tkr_aux.base + cycles_to_ns(tkr_aux, cycles);

        // Should probably be in a separate storage and not in the
        // timekeeper itself, but that's an implementation detail.
        aux_tk->mono_ref.offset = aux_now - mono_now;
        aux_tk->mono_ref.mono_base = mono_now;
        aux_tk->mono_ref.mult = tkr_mono.mult - tkr_aux.mult;
        aux_tk->mono_ref.shift = tkr_mono.shift.
    
This works because both are guaranteed to have the same shift value as
that is never changed during the life time of a clocksource.

Scaled math is fun, isn't it?

How does that get propagated to hrtimers?

struct tk_offsets {
	ktime_t		offs_real;
        ....
	ktime_t		offs_tai;

        struct mono_ref	aux_mono_ref[N];
};

This has to be updated and the aux_clock_offs_seq incremented when:

  1) The AUX clock is set

  2) When the resulting calculation drifts apart significantly

#1 is obvious and #2 can be easily checked on every tick update:

        cycles = read_clock();
        mono_now = tkr_mono.base + cycles_to_ns(tkr_mono, cycles);
        aux_now = tkr_aux.base + cycles_to_ns(tkr_aux, cycles);

        // Should probably be in a separate storage and not in the
        // timekeeper itself, but that's an implementation detail.
        aux_tk->mono_ref.offset = aux_now - mono_now;
        aux_tk->mono_ref.mono_base = mono_now;
        aux_tk->mono_ref.mult = tkr_mono.mult - tkr_aux.mult;
        aux_tk->mono_ref.shift = tkr_mono.shift.

        // Calculate the deviation 
        delta = mono_now - tk_offsets.aux_mono_refs[$ID].mono_base;
        adj = (delta * tk_offsets.aux_mono_refs[$ID].mult) >> tk_offsets.aux_mono_refs[$ID].shift;
        aux_clc = mono_now + adj;
        if (abs(aux_clc - aux_now) > 25)
        	tkr_aux.offset_needs_update = true;

I picked 25ns as an arbitrary threshold as that should be good enough to
be non-observable at the end.

The update period obviously depends on the changes of tk_mono.mult and
tk_aux.mult. If they do not change at all, then it's determined by the
rounding errors of the scaled math. Otherwise the following formula
applies (plus/minus sign errors I did not check and are at the end
irrelevant):

        tAux_offs_ref = tdelta_mono + tdelta_mono * (fmono - faux_offs_ref)
        tAux_curr     = tdelta_mono + tdelta_mono * (fmono - faux_curr)
==>
        tAux_offs_ref = tdelta_mono * (1 + fmono - faux_offs_ref)
        tAux_curr     = tdelta_mono * (1 + fmono - faux_curr)

        tAux_delta    = tAux_offs_ref - tAux_curr
==>
        tAux_delta    = tdelta_mono * (1 + fmono - faux_offs_ref - 1 - fmono - faux_curr)
        tAux_delta    = tdelta_mono * (faux_curr - faux_offs_ref)
==>
        tdelta_mono   = tAux_delta / (faux_curr - faux_offs_ref)

As this cares only about the point where it runs out of the tAux_delta
window, this becomes:

        tdelta_mono   = tAux_delta / abs(faux_curr - faux_offs_ref)

Converting that back to scaled math:

        tdelta_mono   = (tAux_delta << shift) / abs(tkr_aux.mult - tkr_aux.orig_mult)
        
where orig_mult is the multiplier at the time tk_offset.aux_mono_ref got
updated. It does not really matter whether the AUX or the MONO
multiplier is updated. What matters is the relative change and all of it
is determined by the shift value.

So with tAux_delta = 25ns this results in the following update periods
for abs(mult - orig_mult) with shift = 24, which is what e.g. TSC ends up
with usually.

1  :      419.430400 ms
2  :      209.715200 ms
3  :      139.810133 ms
4  :      104.857600 ms
5  :       83.886080 ms
6  :       69.905067 ms
7  :       59.918629 ms
8  :       52.428800 ms
9  :       46.603378 ms
10 :       41.943040 ms

As the usual NTP updates are in the +/- 1 range for both clocks and not
very frequent once the PLL has stabilized, this reduces the update time
significantly. That matters as we really want to avoid shuffling cache
lines around for nothing especially on large systems.

This update behaviour should be instrumented somehow during testing so
we can reason about it.

We could then even go a step further and ignore the AUX offset changes
when a CPU does not have a AUX timer queued. When the first AUX timer is
queued then this needs to update before evaluating the expiry time and
keep it updated until the last AUX timer vanished. But that's an
orthogonal optimization once the dust has settled on the general
approach.

